#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e68ae67-2175-55a3-ac4a-117a340a32a4" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve to a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04a10cbc-62bc-52ef-9573-9bb8535117b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454915} {:answer/id #uuid "2e04cc6e-105d-58a6-b535-adb2aa80e8fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454971} {:answer/id #uuid "5374193f-6c53-5136-b249-4387a6f7f018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455054} {:answer/id #uuid "9a399ff3-ac04-580d-8f27-2c4ed2b64de5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455184} {:answer/id #uuid "eb4fc49c-efc5-5ad5-9f98-7f2015d47df2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04a10cbc-62bc-52ef-9573-9bb8535117b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454915} :answer-field/id #uuid "98645239-f570-5bdc-ae35-ee32a0ef62bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "50247cc3-8637-5b67-95c3-4cdd3dd3329e" :question/math-academy-id "q-76582" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e68ae67-2175-55a3-ac4a-117a340a32a4" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve to a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c0f1a3a-2816-584e-a58f-1a944b890014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455004} {:answer/id #uuid "59542311-3beb-5ba9-afe8-6ee4234a603f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455059} {:answer/id #uuid "65217f58-febb-5f88-af93-f698f3e104ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455086} {:answer/id #uuid "aac6174e-2cde-5d16-8869-626f99dc3227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455201} {:answer/id #uuid "d5463613-7aa5-5ac0-8e87-c4d1223952c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5463613-7aa5-5ac0-8e87-c4d1223952c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455273} :answer-field/id #uuid "157407e8-4b6e-5f9e-8bc5-3a22df95510b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "22016566-6911-5c05-b305-606b27ad2ee0" :question/math-academy-id "q-76584" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{3}\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt{3})=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455497 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e68ae67-2175-55a3-ac4a-117a340a32a4" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve to a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42db5338-1e39-52a9-9b3f-1945f45b8bf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455015} {:answer/id #uuid "66a47cde-b229-5742-a3c9-cd9a46d82b71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455091} {:answer/id #uuid "8942f4a7-3e21-56af-9bb4-658bf93f6604" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455151} {:answer/id #uuid "ad686914-2f26-5181-bfe3-df5b8db65ff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455207} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ea4357-cdc0-5451-a938-bd13cfa04e3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8942f4a7-3e21-56af-9bb4-658bf93f6604" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455151} :answer-field/id #uuid "300a6c45-5c56-5b70-8220-7a36e97b7d82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce434154-3398-5488-977f-b3adb13e5b25" :question/math-academy-id "q-76607" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e68ae67-2175-55a3-ac4a-117a340a32a4" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve to a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cc074f0-8d82-55d9-960a-7332cb2a9aba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454931} {:answer/id #uuid "1a14529d-3768-5610-b079-b36a0abcae5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454945} {:answer/id #uuid "3dabd53e-5bb6-5035-b84f-4f492fd16bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455006} {:answer/id #uuid "705ccca2-e01f-5eb0-be9c-6619697c9f9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455112} {:answer/id #uuid "e0fc3cdf-cbc6-5629-a612-73348098ca7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455291}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a14529d-3768-5610-b079-b36a0abcae5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454945} :answer-field/id #uuid "30132071-c6bc-5d04-908b-c84e3cfc46ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bcf9f8c6-5d3c-5211-90e2-bddb94921272" :question/math-academy-id "q-76609" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}xy(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=1\\,$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $-\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=xy(1-{x}^{2})$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot (1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(1)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345254 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152162 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee6e8ead-8186-5afe-bd66-d11c55b65dd4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68904330-d856-56ab-ab89-95598aff7f05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343132} {:answer/id #uuid "7643286e-f9ff-585d-9aca-43eda7f2b710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343260} {:answer/id #uuid "ccc1b583-ed2c-5e36-a6b5-501f45d92300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi -3}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344043} {:answer/id #uuid "eba40804-2b69-5b03-8ebc-fcc8b5946e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi +1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344303} {:answer/id #uuid "ec2c9374-53c5-5786-8c80-f18bc786264b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi -3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccc1b583-ed2c-5e36-a6b5-501f45d92300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi -3}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344043} :answer-field/id #uuid "e39c6e76-f79f-5404-bd14-1344e4f9c50e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21e9d3c4-49da-5fcb-92fe-c05185c24062" :question/math-academy-id "q-76767" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y$ along the polar curve $r=1$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(1)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\theta  & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}\\theta =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for the integration by parts, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {|}_{0}^{\\pi /3}-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{6}-0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{6}+(\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi -3}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
